  "canon:pmid": "36077103",
  "canon:title": "The Role of Transcription Factor PPAR-gamma in the Pathogenesis of Psoriasis, Skin Cells, and Immune Cells.",
  "canon:abstract": "The peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor PPAR-gamma is one of three PPAR nuclear receptors that act as ligand-activated transcription factors. In immune cells, the skin, and other organs, PPAR-gamma regulates lipid, glucose, and amino acid metabolism. The receptor translates nutritional, pharmacological, and metabolic stimuli into the changes in gene expression. The activation of PPAR-gamma promotes cell differentiation, reduces the proliferation rate, and modulates the immune response. In the skin, PPARs also contribute to the functioning of the skin barrier. Since we know that the route from identification to the registration of drugs is long and expensive, PPAR-gamma agonists already approved for other diseases may also represent a high interest for psoriasis. In this review, we discuss the role of PPAR-gamma in the activation, differentiation, and proliferation of skin and immune cells affected by psoriasis and in contributing to the pathogenesis of the disease. We also evaluate whether the agonists of PPAR-gamma may become one of the therapeutic options to suppress the inflammatory response in lesional psoriatic skin and decrease the influence of comorbidities associated with psoriasis.",
  "canon:text": "The Role of Transcription Factor PPAR-gamma in the Pathogenesis of Psoriasis, Skin Cells, and Immune Cells. The peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor PPAR-gamma is one of three PPAR nuclear receptors that act as ligand-activated transcription factors. In immune cells, the skin, and other organs, PPAR-gamma regulates lipid, glucose, and amino acid metabolism. The receptor translates nutritional, pharmacological, and metabolic stimuli into the changes in gene expression. The activation of PPAR-gamma promotes cell differentiation, reduces the proliferation rate, and modulates the immune response. In the skin, PPARs also contribute to the functioning of the skin barrier. Since we know that the route from identification to the registration of drugs is long and expensive, PPAR-gamma agonists already approved for other diseases may also represent a high interest for psoriasis. In this review, we discuss the role of PPAR-gamma in the activation, differentiation, and proliferation of skin and immune cells affected by psoriasis and in contributing to the pathogenesis of the disease. We also evaluate whether the agonists of PPAR-gamma may become one of the therapeutic options to suppress the inflammatory response in lesional psoriatic skin and decrease the influence of comorbidities associated with psoriasis.",
